values ('b0c1d2e3-4f5a-4b98-9c79-8d9e0f1a2b88', 'mathematical-economics', 'Mathematical Economics', 'The curriculum includes unconstrained and constrained optimization, Lagrange multipliers, comparative statics, linear algebra, dynamic optimization, difference and differential equations, dynamic programming, game theory formalization, and applications to micro and macro models. Mathematical Economics gives students the formal toolkit used to state, solve, and interpret economic models. The syllabus expands optimization, equilibrium, dynamics, and game-theoretic methods while keeping economic interpretation central.');

